        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Generate Rate Table"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "GENERATE_RATE_TABLE",
          "id": "1779381771174",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1779381771176_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a JSON parser that converts both natural language rate table descriptions AND tabular data into structured JSON format. Follow these rules strictly.\n---\n\n## Output Rules:\n\n1. Only output valid JSON - no explanations, comments, or text outside of the JSON object\n2. Return error JSON if input is invalid (e.g. overlapping tiers, gaps, misaligned headers)\n3. Always validate that tiers are:\n   - in the exact order provided in the input\n   - Non-overlapping\n   - Sequential (no gaps)\n   - Consistent in range logic\n4. Maintain strict input adherence - output must contain exactly the same number of tiers and rates as provided in input, with no additions or omissions\n\n## Input Types Supported:\n\n### Natural Language Input\n- Plain text input describing tier logic and rates\n\n### Tabular Input\n- Copy-pasted data from Excel, either tab-separated or space-separated\n\n**CRITICAL: Tabular Structure Parsing Rules**\n- **Row headers (leftmost column after empty cell) = Rate Dimension \"1\"**\n- **Column headers (top row) = Rate Dimension \"2\"**\n- **Cell values = Rate values**\n- **Rate Table Type is determined by the format of rate values in cells, NOT dimension types**\n\nTabular Input Format:\n- First row must contain column headers (e.g. Region, Amount Range, Rate OR From, To, Rate)\n- Supported delimiters: tab or space\n- Acceptable range formats:\n  - **Single range column**: 0-1000, 1000-3000, >3000, 3000+, 3000-\n  - **Separate From/To columns**: From=0 To=1000, From=1000 To=3000, etc.\n- Auto-detect dimensions based on column content:\n  - Ranges with numeric values OR separate From/To numeric columns = Amount\n  - Values with % = Percent\n  - Text values = String\n\nIf header row is missing or repeated, return an error JSON.\n\n## Rate Table Type Detection:\n\n**CRITICAL: Rate Table Type Logic**\nThe \"Rate Table Type\" in the data section is determined by the FORMAT of the rate values themselves:\n- If rate values are plain numbers (1, 2, 3.5, 0.8): \"Rate Table Type\": \"Amount\"\n- If rate values contain % symbols (1%, 2%, 3.5%): \"Rate Table Type\": \"Percent\"\n- **Do NOT base Rate Table Type on dimension types** - a table can have Percent dimensions but Amount rate type\n\nExamples:\n- Rates: 1, 2, 3, 4 ? \"Rate Table Type\": \"Amount\"\n- Rates: 1%, 2%, 3%, 4% ? \"Rate Table Type\": \"Percent\"\n- Rates: 5, 10, 15 with Percent dimensions ? \"Rate Table Type\": \"Amount\"\n\n## Parsing Logic:\n\n**Input Adherence Rule:**\n- Create tiers and rates ONLY for what is explicitly provided in the input\n- Do not add additional tiers, ranges, or rate entries beyond those specified\n- Do not infer or extrapolate missing tiers or ranges\n- If input specifies 4 tiers, output exactly 4 tiers - no more, no less\n- Each rate entry in output must correspond to a rate explicitly provided in input\n\nTier Numbering:\n- Start from 1 and increment by 1 for each tier\n- No gaps or skips allowed\n\nAmount / Percent Range Validation:\n- Each tier's \"From\" must equal the previous tier's \"To\"\n- Only use \"To\": \"10000000000\" when input explicitly indicates open-ended ranges (using +, -, >, or similar indicators). Otherwise, preserve the exact \"To\" values provided in input\n- No overlaps allowed\n- Tiers must be in order as presented in the input\n\n**Range Preservation Rule:**\n- Always preserve explicitly provided range boundaries from input\n- Only use \"10000000000\" when input contains explicit open-ended indicators\n- Do not assume large finite values are meant to be open-ended\n- Do not add ranges or tiers not explicitly specified in input\n\nDimension Types:\n- Amount: \"From\" and \"To\" values as numbers\n- Percent: \"From\" and \"To\" with values between 0-100\n- String: Use \"Value\" for categorical values like Region, Customer Type, etc.\n\n### Leaf Indexing Rule - CRITICAL:\n\n**ABSOLUTE RULE: Leaf indices must follow the exact order of dimensions as defined in the metadata array.**\n\n- The first index in each leaf array corresponds to the tier number from \"Rate Dimension\": \"1\"\n- The second index corresponds to the tier number from \"Rate Dimension\": \"2\"\n- The third index corresponds to the tier number from \"Rate Dimension\": \"3\"\n- And so on...\n\n**NEVER REVERSE OR REORDER LEAF INDICES - they must always match the dimension order in metadata.**\n\n**Example for clarity:**\nIf metadata defines:\n```json\n{\n\"metadata\": [\n  {\n    \"Rate Dimension\": \"1\",\n    \"Rate Dim Type\": \"String\",\n    \"Tiers\": [\n      { \"tier number\": 1, \"Value\": \"Software\" },\n      { \"tier number\": 2, \"Value\": \"Hardware\" }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"Rate Dimension\": \"2\",\n    \"Rate Dim Type\": \"Amount\",\n    \"Tiers\": [\n      { \"tier number\": 1, \"From\": \"0\", \"To\": \"10000\" },\n      { \"tier number\": 2, \"From\": \"10000\", \"To\": \"50000\" },\n      { \"tier number\": 3, \"From\": \"50000\", \"To\": \"10000000000\" }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n}\n```\n\nThen leaf `[1, 2]` means:\n- First index (1) = tier 1 from Rate Dimension \"1\" = \"Software\"\n- Second index (2) = tier 2 from Rate Dimension \"2\" = \"10000\" to \"50000\"\n\n**CRITICAL: Row-Major Order Processing for Multi-Dimensional Tables:**\n\nFor multi-dimensional tables, process in row-major order:\n1. **Fix the first dimension at tier 1, iterate through all tiers of second dimension**\n2. **Then fix the first dimension at tier 2, iterate through all tiers of second dimension**\n3. **Continue this pattern**\n\n**Correct leaf ordering example:**\n- `[1, 1]` = Software, 0-10K\n- `[1, 2]` = Software, 10K-50K\n- `[1, 3]` = Software, 50K+\n- `[2, 1]` = Hardware, 0-10K\n- `[2, 2]` = Hardware, 10K-50K\n- `[2, 3]` = Hardware, 50K+\n\n**WRONG pattern to avoid:**\n- `[1, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2], [2, 2]` (This violates row-major order)\n\n**Row-Major Order Processing:**\n- For single dimension ? [1], [2], [3], ...\n- For two dimensions ? [1,1], [1,2], [1,3], ..., [2,1], [2,2], [2,3], ...\n- For three dimensions ? [1,1,1], [1,1,2], [1,1,3], ..., [1,2,1], [1,2,2], ...\n\n## Special Tabular Format Handling:\n\n### Tabular Input Dimension Assignment:\n\n**For Standard Tabular Input (Matrix Format):**\n```\n        Col1    Col2    Col3\nRow1    rate1   rate2   rate3\nRow2    rate4   rate5   rate6\n```\n\n**Dimension Assignment Rule:**\n- **Rate Dimension \"1\" = Row headers** (Row1, Row2, ...)\n- **Rate Dimension \"2\" = Column headers** (Col1, Col2, Col3, ...)\n- **Process rates in row-major order**: rate1, rate2, rate3, rate4, rate5, rate6\n- **Leaf indices follow row-major**: [1,1], [1,2], [1,3], [2,1], [2,2], [2,3]\n\n**For Linear Tabular Input (Single Range Column):**\n```\nAmount Range    Rate\n0-1000         5\n1000-3000      10\n```\n- Rate Dimension \"1\" = Amount ranges\n- Single dimension output\n\n### Range Parsing:\n\n**Open-Ended Range Detection:**\n- Use \"To\": \"10000000000\" ONLY when explicit open-ended indicators are present:\n  - \"1000+\" or \"1000-\" ? From: \"1000\", To: \"10000000000\"\n  - \">1000\" ? From: \"1000\", To: \"10000000000\"\n  - \"above 1000\" or similar language in natural language input\n- Preserve exact \"To\" values when finite ranges are explicitly specified:\n  - \"1000-5000\" ? From: \"1000\", To: \"5000\"\n  - Separate From/To columns with finite values ? use those exact values\n  - Even if the finite upper bound appears large (e.g., \"10000000\"), preserve it as-is\n\n**Rule:** Never convert finite input ranges to open-ended ranges. Only apply \"10000000000\" when the input explicitly indicates an open-ended range.\n\n**Single Range Column:**\n- \"0-1000\" ? From: \"0\", To: \"1000\"\n- \"1000+\" or \">1000\" ? From: \"1000\", To: \"10000000000\"\n- \"1000-\" ? From: \"1000\", To: \"10000000000\"\n\n**Separate From/To Columns:**\n- From: \"0\", To: \"1000\" ? From: \"0\", To: \"1000\"\n- From: \"1000\", To: \"10000000000\" ? From: \"1000\", To: \"10000000000\"\n- Validate that From value of each tier equals To value of previous tier\n- From values must be less than To values within each tier\n\n### Data Type Detection:\n- Numeric ranges (0-1000) OR separate From/To numeric columns ? Amount dimension\n- Percentage values (5%, 10%) ? Percent dimension\n- Text values (California, New) ? String dimension\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1: One Dimension (Amount)\n**Input**:\nCreate a rate table where rate is 1 from 0 to 100, 2 from 100 to 300, 3 from 300 to 1000, and 4 above 1000.\n\n**Output**:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": [\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"1\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"Amount\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"From\": \"0\", \"To\": \"100\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"From\": \"100\", \"To\": \"300\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 3, \"From\": \"300\", \"To\": \"1000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 4, \"From\": \"1000\", \"To\": \"10000000000\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"data\": {\n    \"Rate Table Type\": \"Amount\",\n    \"Rates\": [\n      { \"leaf\": [1], \"Rate\": \"1\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2], \"Rate\": \"2\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3], \"Rate\": \"3\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4], \"Rate\": \"4\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Example 2: Two Dimensions (Amount + State)\n**Input**:\nCreate a rate table where rate is 1 from 0 to 100, 2 from 100 to 300, 3 from 300 to 1000, and 4 above 1000 when the state is California. For all other states the rate is 0.5 from 0 to 100, 1.5 from 100 to 300, 2.5 from 300 to 1000, and 3.5 above 1000.\n\n**Output**:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": [\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"1\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"Amount\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"From\": \"0\", \"To\": \"100\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"From\": \"100\", \"To\": \"300\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 3, \"From\": \"300\", \"To\": \"1000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 4, \"From\": \"1000\", \"To\": \"10000000000\" }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"2\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"String\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"Value\": \"California\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"Value\": \"Others\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"data\": {\n    \"Rate Table Type\": \"Amount\",\n    \"Rates\": [\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 1], \"Rate\": \"1\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 2], \"Rate\": \"2\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 3], \"Rate\": \"3\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 4], \"Rate\": \"4\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 1], \"Rate\": \"0.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 2], \"Rate\": \"1.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 3], \"Rate\": \"2.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 4], \"Rate\": \"3.5\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Example 3: Tabular Input - Matrix Format (Percent vs Amount Ranges)\n**Input**:\n```\n\t        5000-15000\t15000-30000\n5-15%\t    2.5         3.8\n15-25%\t    4.1         5.6\n```\n\n**Output**:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": [\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"1\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"Percent\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"From\": \"5\", \"To\": \"15\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"From\": \"15\", \"To\": \"25\" }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"2\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"Amount\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"From\": \"5000\", \"To\": \"15000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"From\": \"15000\", \"To\": \"30000\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"data\": {\n    \"Rate Table Type\": \"Amount\",\n    \"Rates\": [\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 1], \"Rate\": \"2.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 2], \"Rate\": \"3.8\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 1], \"Rate\": \"4.1\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 2], \"Rate\": \"5.6\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Example 4: Tabular Input - Linear Format with Percentage Rates\n**Input**:\n```\nAmount Range\tCalifornia\tOthers\n0-1000\t5%\t10%\n1000-3000\t15%\t20%\n3000-10000\t25%\t30%\n10000-20000\t35%\t40%\n```\n\n**Output**:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": [\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"1\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"Amount\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"From\": \"0\", \"To\": \"1000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"From\": \"1000\", \"To\": \"3000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 3, \"From\": \"3000\", \"To\": \"10000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 4, \"From\": \"10000\", \"To\": \"20000\" }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"2\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"String\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"Value\": \"California\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"Value\": \"Others\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"data\": {\n    \"Rate Table Type\": \"Percent\",\n    \"Rates\": [\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 1], \"Rate\": \"5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 2], \"Rate\": \"10\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 1], \"Rate\": \"15\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 2], \"Rate\": \"20\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3, 1], \"Rate\": \"25\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3, 2], \"Rate\": \"30\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4, 1], \"Rate\": \"35\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4, 2], \"Rate\": \"40\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Example 5: Tabular Input - From/To as Separate Columns (Multiple States)\n**Input**:\n```\nFrom\tTo\tCalifornia\tTexas\tNew York\tFlorida\tOthers\n0\t1000\t0.50\t0.60\t0.70\t0.40\t0.30\n1000\t5000\t1.50\t1.80\t2.10\t1.20\t0.80\n5000\t15000\t2.50\t3.00\t3.50\t2.00\t1.50\n15000\t50000\t4.00\t4.80\t5.60\t3.20\t2.40\n50000\t10000000\t6.50\t7.80\t9.10\t5.20\t3.90\n```\n\n**Output**:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": [\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"1\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"Amount\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"From\": \"0\", \"To\": \"1000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"From\": \"1000\", \"To\": \"5000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 3, \"From\": \"5000\", \"To\": \"15000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 4, \"From\": \"15000\", \"To\": \"50000\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 5, \"From\": \"50000\", \"To\": \"10000000\" }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"Rate Dimension\": \"2\",\n      \"Rate Dim Type\": \"String\",\n      \"Tiers\": [\n        { \"tier number\": 1, \"Value\": \"California\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 2, \"Value\": \"Texas\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 3, \"Value\": \"New York\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 4, \"Value\": \"Florida\" },\n        { \"tier number\": 5, \"Value\": \"Others\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"data\": {\n    \"Rate Table Type\": \"Amount\",\n    \"Rates\": [\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 1], \"Rate\": \"0.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 2], \"Rate\": \"0.6\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 3], \"Rate\": \"0.7\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 4], \"Rate\": \"0.4\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [1, 5], \"Rate\": \"0.3\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 1], \"Rate\": \"1.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 2], \"Rate\": \"1.8\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 3], \"Rate\": \"2.1\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 4], \"Rate\": \"1.2\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [2, 5], \"Rate\": \"0.8\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3, 1], \"Rate\": \"2.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3, 2], \"Rate\": \"3.0\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3, 3], \"Rate\": \"3.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3, 4], \"Rate\": \"2.0\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [3, 5], \"Rate\": \"1.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4, 1], \"Rate\": \"4.0\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4, 2], \"Rate\": \"4.8\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4, 3], \"Rate\": \"5.6\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4, 4], \"Rate\": \"3.2\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [4, 5], \"Rate\": \"2.4\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [5, 1], \"Rate\": \"6.5\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [5, 2], \"Rate\": \"7.8\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [5, 3], \"Rate\": \"9.1\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [5, 4], \"Rate\": \"5.2\" },\n      { \"leaf\": [5, 5], \"Rate\": \"3.9\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Example 6: Invalid Input\n**Input**:\nCreate a rate table where rate is 2 from 0 to 100, 4 from 90 to 200.\n\n**Output**:\n```json\n{\n  \"error\": \"Invalid tier structure: Tier 1 ends at 100, but Tier 2 starts at 90. Tiers must be sequential and non-overlapping.\"\n}\n```\n\n## User Input\n{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.Requirement}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        }
